1) Git is the local distributed version installed on dev machines used to track changes in the 
local repository and interact with github. gitHub is the cloud platform used by multiple devs to 
work on the same project, github interacts with git on local machines to enable devs to pull and
push from/to the server. 

2) git add <file> only adds the single file to the staging area (sort of like modified buffer) 
where changes are temporarily stored. git add . adds all files that have been changed to the staging
area. git add -A adds all files that have been changed but also includes deletions across the entire
repo.

3) git push takes the commited changes from the local git repo and merges them with the gitHub cloud
repo git pull takes the changes from the gitHub coud repo that the local repo doesnt yet have, adds changes + new files
and reconciles the differences.

4) fetch takes the changes from gitHub cloud and saves them to a modified buffer/staging area but
does not change any working code on the local machine. Git pull does the same but mergest the 
staging area with the local repo, immediately modifying local code without chance to preview changes
in contrast to fetch.
 
